Hi All,

I have a question regarding a conditional multinomial model I am writing. My models are for point count data (abundance counts of birds species) where we split up our 8-minute point counts into two intervals of differing lengths, 5 minutes, then 3 minutes. My question is, is what I have written below the correct formulation for the multinomial part of the model? I have seen a few different ways of formulating different interval lengths, and have got varying results when running simulations. My code below reflects examples from Kery and Royle (2016, ch. 7.6 and 7.7):

In this model, there are "M" sites, visited "a" times per season, with "k" intervals. This code is written in JAGS.

  for (i in 1:M) {  #loops over sites
    for(a in 1:3) { #loops over visits

    pi[i,1,a] <- 1- pow((1-p[i,a]), 5) #prob of seeing bird in first interval (5 min)
    pi[i,2,a] <- 1- pow(1- p[i,a], 3)  #prob of seeing bird in second interval (3 min)
    pi0[i,a] <- 1 - (pi[i,1,a] + pi[i,2,a]) #prob of never seeing bird
    pcap[i,a]<- 1 - pi0[i,a]  #total capture prob
    for (k in 1:2){
      pic[i,k,a] <- pi[i,k,a] / pcap[i,a]  #conditional capture prob
    }
  }
}

My question is mainly concerned with the formula for the probability of seeing the bird in the second interval ( 1- pow(1- p[i,a], 3) ).

Happy to provide a reproducible example if that would help. Thank you in advance for any suggestions or advice.

Best,
Martha

