Hi Zach,

Weighting the posterior would be pretty easy to implement in Stan by defining increments of the lpd like so:

target += weight[i] * (log(psi[i])+bernoulli_lmpf(y[i, ]|p)) // or,  weight[i]*log_sum_exp(log(psi[i])+bernoulli_lmpf(y[i, ]|p), log1m(psi[i]))

I *think* that for Nimble, building a custom distribution might be the way to go (maybe building off some of the distributions associated with NimbleEcology?).  If using BUGS/JAGS, first thought is that maybe using the zeros/ones tricks is the way to go (i.e., phi[i]<- -log(L[i]) * weight[i]+C; zeros[i]~dpois(phi[i]))?

All this said, what seems more commonplace within Bayesian approaches--to the limited extent that I've read about this--is to try to model the sampling selection process jointly with the state process and observation process. I think there's a paper by Paul Conn and others in MEE (2017?) that goes over preferential sampling. When it comes out, Volume 2 of the AHM (Kery and Royle) book might have an example for dynamic occupancy models?  I have no idea whether weighting/penalizing the log posterior density is viewed as inappropriate, or just inefficient relative to the alternatives. Would be interested to hear from somebody who knows better.

John

