Hi Mario,

I'm not working on gecko samplings but on fish sampling and metapopulation estimation under a bayesian framework.

I've done some research to find "state of the art" publications on modeling methods. Here are some publications i suggest you to read :
- Hierarchical Models of Animal Abundance and Occurrence, J. Andrew ROYLE and Robert M. DORAZIO
- HIERARCHICAL SPATIAL MODELS OF ABUNDANCE AND OCCURRENCE FROM IMPERFECT SURVEY DATA,
J. ANDREW ROYLE, MARC KERY, ROLAND GAUTIER, HANS SCHMID
- A hierarchical model combining distance sampling and time removal to estimate detection probability during avian point counts
Authors: Amundson, Courtney L., Royle, J. Andrew, and Handel, Colleen M.

Also i suggest you to perform a different approach to model your count data and consider the "sites" you sampled as subdivisions of a whole habitat and try to modelise how your population of geckos is distributed between these diffetents compartments. To do so you could use DIreichlet distribution. This is the solution i'm working on currently. I'm still new with it but it seems very promising. Here are some articles to read :
- http://phyletica.org/dirichlet-process/ --> a quick but useful introduction to dirichlet distribution for ecology
- Analysing continuous proportions in ecology and evolution: A practical introduction to beta and Dirichlet regression Jacob C. Douma, James T. Weedon(a good start for understanding the underlying concepts)
- Dirichlet-multinomial modelling outperforms alternatives for analysis of microbiome and other ecological count data Joshua G. Harrison, W. John Calder, Vivaswat Shastry, and C. Alex Buerkle1

Finally, when modelizing the effect of covariates on biological parameters of a model, i prefer to start with a model without covariates and do post-hoc analysis on residuals of the model or aother biological paramters versus covariates. If such analysis seems to highlight correlation or any kind of effect on parameters/residuals, then i add it to the model. Also, sometimes environnemental data need to be "transformed" or at least "normalized" before any statistiscal analysis.

Hope it helps !

Good luck with your data !

